---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { aboutJsonLd } from '../lib/seo';
const description = "localdobe is a set of free PDF tools that run entirely in your browser — no uploads, no watermarks, no signup. Here's why it exists and how it works.";
---
<Base title="About localdobe — free, private PDF tools" {description} canonicalPath="/about"
  jsonLd={[aboutJsonLd(description)]}>
  <article class="prose prose-slate mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 pt-12 dark:prose-invert">
    <h1>About localdobe</h1>
    <p>
      localdobe is a small collection of PDF tools — merge, split, compress, edit,
      watermark, protect, unlock, and signature validation — that all run locally in your
      browser. No file you work with ever touches a server.
    </p>
    <h2>Why this exists</h2>
    <p>
      Working with PDFs has long meant choosing between two compromises. You could
      install a heavyweight desktop suite — a multi-gigabyte download with update
      prompts, bundled extras, and the features you actually need locked behind a
      subscription. Or you could use a "free" online tool that requires uploading your
      document — often a contract, a bank statement, a medical form — to a server you
      know nothing about, with retention policies you'll never read, plus paywalls,
      watermarks, and page caps for good measure.
    </p>
    <p>
      localdobe removes the choice: there's nothing to install <em>and</em> nothing to
      upload. Modern browsers can do this work themselves, so localdobe just does the
      work on your machine instead of asking you to trust ours — free, with no watermarks
      and no account.
    </p>
    <h2>The tech</h2>
    <p>
      The tools are built on trusted, open-source PDF software that runs directly inside
      your browser tab, the same kind of professional PDF technology used by developers
      everywhere — one library handles document assembly like merging and page
      manipulation, another powers compression, encryption, and watermarking, and a third
      renders pages so you can preview and edit them. None of it requires a plugin, an
      account, or a network round-trip for your file data. If you're curious about the
      details, our <a href="/blog/webassembly-pdf-tools/">blog post on the technology behind
      it</a> goes deeper.
    </p>
    <h2>Being honest about it</h2>
    <p>
      We'd rather you understand how this works than just trust a badge that says
      "private." Read the <a href="/privacy/">privacy page</a> for the specifics on what
      does and doesn't leave your device, or check the <a href="/blog/">blog</a> for
      deeper dives into individual tools.
    </p>
    <h2>Open source</h2>
    <p>
      Trust shouldn't rely on our word alone, so localdobe is fully open source. All of the
      code — under the permissive MIT License — is published in a public repository you can
      read, audit, or fork:{" "}
      <a href="https://github.com/murugu-21/localdobe" class="font-medium text-accent underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-ink">
        github.com/murugu-21/localdobe
      </a>
      . The PDF engine it runs on, <a href="https://github.com/pdfcpu/pdfcpu">pdfcpu</a>, is
      open source too, under the Apache 2.0 License.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
